he introduction will establish why benchmarks assume comparability, explain how items might function differently across model generations, introduce the psychometric DIF framework, lay out the main contributions, and clarify that instability doesn't necessarily mean invalidity.\n\nNow I'm structuring the rest of the paper: covering related work on benchmark contamination and psychometric approaches in NLP, then detailing the MH-DIF methodology with the ETS classification scheme and semi-synthetic validation framework, followed by experiments on temporal DIF patterns, cross-benchmark validation, Simpson's paradox effects, and downstream impact analysis.\n\nWriting the LaTeX document now with careful attention to formatting conventions.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12674,"cache_read_input_tokens":35955,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12674},"output_tokens":11,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"648a2789-75b4-4e54-9d3e-23d488f8eda0","uuid":"c88d5f92-e60c-4c3e-9c16-6ae79b2980dc"}
