{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016fcqQ6ACN7XqSZpsvrj8LY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016reDSRBLbjt9MpxKxPZ1Eq","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T110541/best/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage{times}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{enumitem}\n\n\\title{How Stable Are LLM Benchmarks?\\\\Psychometric DIF Analysis of 5,227 Models Reveals\\\\31\\% Temporal Item Instability}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n%% ========================================================================\n%% ABSTRACT\n%% ========================================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\nWhen we compare Large Language Model (LLM) scores across time, we implicitly assume that benchmark items measure the same construct for all model cohorts.\nWe test this assumption using Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis on the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), the largest such analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\nWe find that 31.3\\% of items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS C class) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts, a rate 174$\\times$ above random-split controls (0.18\\%) and consistent across six benchmarks (17.9\\%--31.3\\%).\nA Simpson's paradox analysis reveals that the na\\\"ive positive association between domain-level accuracy improvement and DIF (OR\\,=\\,1.55) reverses after controlling for item properties (OR\\,=\\,0.73), confirming that instability is predominantly item-specific rather than driven by capability gains.\nWe further demonstrate that the field's standard web-overlap ground truth labels are orthogonal to differential item functioning (enrichment\\,$\\approx$\\,1.0), and provide a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates false positive rate inflation ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000).\nDespite 31\\% instability, MMLU model rankings remain highly stable ($\\rho$\\,$\\approx$\\,0.997), indicating massive measurement redundancy.\nWe propose a DIF-based audit protocol for benchmark maintainers to monitor and respond to temporal item drift.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%% ========================================================================\n%% 1  INTRODUCTION\n%% ========================================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\nLLM evaluation relies on static benchmarks such as MMLU, GSM8K, and ARC-Challenge to track progress across model generations.\nAn implicit assumption underlies every cross-temporal comparison: the items that compose these benchmarks measure the same latent construct with the same difficulty for all models, regardless of when those models were trained.\nIf this assumption fails, score comparisons across model cohorts become ambiguous.\nPsychometrics provides a mature tool for testing this assumption: Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability \\citep{holland1988differential}.\n\nWe apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix \\citep{perlitz2024metabench}, covering 5,227 models evaluated on 12,508 MMLU items.\nThis constitutes the largest DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\nOur central finding is that approximately 31\\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations.\nThis instability is not a statistical artifact (random-split control: 0.18\\%, permutation null: 0.21\\%), not driven by domain-level capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\% of variance explained), and not specific to MMLU (replicated across six benchmarks with C\\% ranging from 17.9\\% to 31.3\\%).\n\nA key framing principle guides our interpretation: \\textbf{instability does not imply invalidity}.\nRemoving all 31\\% of flagged items changes model rankings negligibly ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$), revealing massive measurement redundancy in MMLU.\nDIF flags identify which items' functioning changed most across generations, serving as a diagnostic lens for understanding capability shifts rather than a quality filter for item exclusion.\n\nWe further identify a fundamental gap in the contamination detection literature: web-overlap labels (the field's standard ground truth) measure global exposure, not differential contamination.\nDIF enrichment against these labels is $\\approx$1.0, and we show via semi-synthetic injection that DIF can detect differential contamination ($\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.508 at 50\\% exploitation rate) while web-overlap labels cannot distinguish it.\nWe provide a reusable semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000) that eliminates matching variable contamination.\n\nOur contributions are:\n\\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n\\item \\textbf{Empirical discovery at scale}: 31.3\\% of MMLU items show large temporal DIF, robust across thresholds, six benchmarks, and multiple testing corrections. The instability is item-specific ($\\leq$7.6\\% explained by domain improvement) and does not threaten ranking validity ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$).\n\\item \\textbf{Ground truth meta-critique}: Web-overlap contamination labels are orthogonal to differential item functioning (enrichment\\,$\\approx$\\,1.0). This affects the validity claims of all item-level detectors validated against such labels.\n\\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework}: The $\\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000) provides reusable infrastructure for validating any item-level contamination detector, with demonstrated portability to GSM8K.\n\\item \\textbf{DIF audit protocol}: A concrete protocol for benchmark maintainers specifying monitoring frequency, decision rules, and response options.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n%% ========================================================================\n%% 2  RELATED WORK\n%% ========================================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark Contamination Detection.}\nThe detection of benchmark contamination in LLMs has attracted substantial attention.\nMethods range from n-gram overlap analysis \\citep{dodge2021documenting,brown2020language} to membership inference approaches such as Min-K\\% \\citep{shi2024detecting} and generation-based methods \\citep{golchin2024time,yang2023rethinking}.\nConStat \\citep{dekoninck2024constat} tests whether semantically equivalent item reformulations produce inconsistent responses.\nDVD \\citep{dong2024generalization} examines generation variance under temperature sampling.\nA common thread across these methods is validation against web-overlap or n-gram ground truth labels \\citep{li2024task,deng2024investigating}, which we argue measures global exposure rather than differential contamination.\nOur work does not propose a new detection method but instead characterizes the stability properties of benchmark items and critiques the prevailing validation methodology.\n\n\\paragraph{Psychometric Methods in NLP Evaluation.}\nItem Response Theory (IRT) has been applied to NLP evaluation for difficulty estimation \\citep{lalor2019learning,rodriguez2021evaluation,vania2021comparing}, adaptive testing \\citep{zhuang2023efficiently}, and benchmark analysis \\citep{blebea2024benchmark}.\n\\citet{polo2024tinybenchmarks} use IRT to construct efficient benchmark subsets.\nDIF analysis, while foundational in educational testing \\citep{holland1988differential,zumbo1999handbook}, has seen limited application in NLP.\nOur work bridges this gap by applying MH-DIF at scale to characterize temporal item stability in LLM benchmarks.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark Design and Maintenance.}\nConcerns about benchmark saturation \\citep{kiela2021dynabench}, data contamination \\citep{magar2022data,jacovi2023stop}, and the need for dynamic evaluation \\citep{zhu2024dyval} have motivated new benchmark design paradigms.\n\\citet{perlitz2024metabench} provide the large-scale response matrices that enable our analysis.\nGrowing Pains \\citep{chiang2024chatbot} identifies anchor items for efficient model comparison; our DIF analysis reveals which anchor items may be degrading over time.\n\n%% ========================================================================\n%% 3  METHOD\n%% ========================================================================\n\\section{Method}\n\n\\subsection{Mantel-Haenszel DIF Analysis}\n\nThe Mantel-Haenszel (MH) procedure \\citep{holland1988differential} tests whether an item functions differently across two groups (reference $R$ and focal $F$) after conditioning on a matching variable that measures overall ability.\nFor each item, models are stratified into $K$ ability strata based on their total score.\nWithin each stratum $k$, a $2 \\times 2$ contingency table records correct/incorrect responses for each group.\nThe MH odds ratio is:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\alpha_{\\text{MH}} = \\frac{\\sum_k A_k D_k / N_k}{\\sum_k B_k C_k / N_k}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $A_k, B_k, C_k, D_k$ are the cell counts and $N_k$ is the stratum total.\nThe log-odds ratio $\\Delta_{\\text{MH}} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_{\\text{MH}})$ converts to the ETS delta scale.\nItems are classified by the ETS system: A (negligible, $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| < 1.0$), B (moderate, $1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| < 1.5$), and C (large, $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$).\nWe focus on C-class items as our primary instability indicator and report C\\% (the percentage of items classified as C).\n\nIn our setting, the reference group ($R$) consists of models released before 2024 (the 2023 cohort) and the focal group ($F$) consists of models released in 2024-H1.\nWe use cross-subject total score as the matching variable: for each MMLU subject, a model's matching score is its total correct on all \\emph{other} subjects.\nThis eliminates matching variable contamination (Section~\\ref{sec:cross-subject}).\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\\label{sec:semisynthetic}\n\nTo evaluate DIF detection power under known ground truth, we inject controlled differential contamination into real METABENCH response matrices.\nFor a target subject with $n$ items, we select $\\lfloor p \\cdot n \\rfloor$ ``clean'' items (those with low baseline DIF) and flip incorrect$\\to$correct responses for all focal-group models on these items, simulating exploitation at rate $p$.\nWe measure detection power via $\\Delta$TPR: the increase in true positive rate at the injected items relative to baseline.\nDose-response curves across $p \\in \\{0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\\}$ verify that detection power increases monotonically with contamination intensity.\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Subject Matching}\n\\label{sec:cross-subject}\n\nStandard MH-DIF uses within-subject total score as the matching variable.\nWhen contamination affects a subset of items within the target subject, the matching variable itself becomes inflated for contaminated models, biasing DIF estimates.\nWe replace within-subject matching with cross-subject matching: a model's ability estimate for subject $s$ is its total score on all subjects \\emph{except} $s$.\nThis ensures the matching variable is independent of any contamination in the target subject.\nWe demonstrate empirically that cross-subject matching yields $\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000 across all tested conditions, while within-subject matching inflates FPR by up to +0.186.\n\n%% ========================================================================\n%% 4  EXPERIMENTS\n%% ========================================================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\n\\subsection{Data}\n\nWe use the METABENCH repository \\citep{perlitz2024metabench}, which provides binary response matrices (correct/incorrect) for models evaluated on standardized benchmarks.\nFor MMLU, the matrix contains 5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 items spanning 57 subjects.\nModels are split into a reference cohort (released before 2024-01-01; $N_R = 2{,}891$) and a focal cohort (released 2024-01-01 to 2024-06-30; $N_F = 2{,}336$).\nThe population is 99.98\\% decoder-only architectures (5,226/5,227); excluding the single non-decoder model leaves all DIF results unchanged (Jaccard\\,=\\,1.0).\n\nFor cross-benchmark validation, we additionally analyze WinoGrande, TruthfulQA, ARC-Challenge, GSM8K (1,319 items $\\times$ 6,702 models), and HellaSwag from the same repository.\n\n\\subsection{Temporal DIF on MMLU}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Temporal DIF on MMLU: 2023 vs.\\ 2024-H1 cohorts.}\n\\label{tab:temporal}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Temporal} & \\textbf{Random Split} \\\\\n\\midrule\nC\\% (ETS C, $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$) & 31.3\\% & 0.18\\% \\\\\nEnrichment vs.\\ random & 174$\\times$ & -- \\\\\nSubjects with DIF & 57/57 & -- \\\\\nSubject C\\% range & 20.7--48.4\\% & -- \\\\\nUnweighted subject mean C\\% & 30.0\\% & -- \\\\\nAbility quintile C\\% & 2.9\\% & -- \\\\\nAbility enrichment & 1.19 & -- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:temporal} summarizes our primary result.\nTemporal DIF analysis identifies 31.3\\% of MMLU items as ETS C class ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$), compared to 0.18\\% under random group assignment (174$\\times$ enrichment).\nAll 57 MMLU subjects contain DIF items, with per-subject C\\% ranging from 20.7\\% to 48.4\\% (unweighted mean 30.0\\%).\nA permutation test (1,000 iterations, randomly reassigning temporal labels) yields a null C\\% of 0.21\\%\\,$\\pm$\\,0.09\\%, placing the observed 31.3\\% more than 345 standard deviations above the null ($p < 0.001$; 0 of 1,000 permutations exceed the observed value).\n\n\\paragraph{Threshold robustness.}\nC\\% varies systematically with the ETS threshold: 48.9\\% at $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.0$, 31.3\\% at $\\geq$1.5, 18.3\\% at $\\geq$2.0, and 10.2\\% at $\\geq$2.5.\nThe finding that $>$15\\% of items show instability holds across standard thresholds [1.0, 2.0].\nBenjamini-Hochberg FDR correction at $q = 0.05$ removes only 3 of 3,918 C-class items (31.32\\%\\,$\\to$\\,31.30\\%), confirming that the ETS dual requirement (effect size $+$ significance) provides near-complete implicit multiple-testing protection.\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture homogeneity.}\nMETABENCH MMLU contains 5,226 decoder-only models and 1 non-decoder model (99.98\\% decoder-only).\nExcluding the non-decoder model yields identical DIF flags (Jaccard\\,=\\,1.0, C\\%\\,=\\,31.3\\%).\nArchitecture heterogeneity is a non-issue for this analysis.\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Validation}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Six-benchmark temporal DIF validation (2023 vs.\\ 2024-H1).}\n\\label{tab:crossbench}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Benchmark} & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{Random C\\%} & \\textbf{Enrichment} \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU & 31.3\\% & 0.18\\% & 174$\\times$ \\\\\nWinoGrande & 30.3\\% & 0.32\\% & 95$\\times$ \\\\\nTruthfulQA & 29.7\\% & 0.37\\% & 80$\\times$ \\\\\nARC-Challenge & 26.9\\% & 0.60\\% & 45$\\times$ \\\\\nGSM8K & 18.9\\% & 0.30\\% & 63$\\times$ \\\\\nHellaSwag & 17.9\\% & 0.28\\% & 64$\\times$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:crossbench} extends the temporal DIF analysis to six benchmarks.\nAll six show substantial DIF (C\\% from 17.9\\% to 31.3\\%), with every random control below 1\\% and a minimum enrichment of 45$\\times$ (ARC-Challenge).\nThe variation across benchmarks reflects structural differences: MMLU (57 subjects, 12,508 items) provides the most dimensions for capability shifts to manifest, while GSM8K (single-domain math) and HellaSwag (commonsense NLI with compressed difficulty variance) show lower but still substantial rates.\nThis cross-benchmark consistency rules out MMLU-specific artifacts and establishes temporal item instability as a general property of LLM benchmarks.\n\n\\subsection{Simpson's Paradox in Ability Confounding}\n\\label{sec:simpsons}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Logistic regression of DIF flag on accuracy improvement. OR: odds ratio per 0.10 increase in predictor; Simpson's paradox reverses the association after controlling for item properties.}\n\\label{tab:simpsons}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Predictor(s)} & \\textbf{OR} & \\textbf{95\\% CI} & \\textbf{McF.\\ $R^2$} \\\\\n\\midrule\nM1 & Domain $\\Delta$acc & 1.55 & [1.19, 2.03] & 0.0007 \\\\\nM2 & Domain $\\Delta$acc & \\textbf{0.73} & -- & 0.0423 \\\\\n   & $+$ CTT difficulty & 0.78 & -- & \\\\\n   & $+$ CTT discrimination & 1.51 & -- & \\\\\n\\midrule\nM3 & Item $\\Delta$acc & 1.08 & [1.51, 2.96] & 0.0012 \\\\\nM4 & Item $\\Delta$acc & 0.92 & -- & 0.0434 \\\\\n   & $+$ CTT difficulty & 0.77 & -- & \\\\\n   & $+$ CTT discrimination & 1.54 & -- & \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nA natural concern is that 31\\% instability simply reflects models ``getting better.''\nTable~\\ref{tab:simpsons} tests this via logistic regression predicting DIF C-class membership.\nModel~1, using only domain-level accuracy improvement as predictor, shows a positive association (OR\\,=\\,1.55): subjects with larger 2024-vs-2023 improvement do have more DIF items.\nHowever, Model~2 adds item-level difficulty and discrimination as controls, and the association \\emph{reverses} (OR\\,=\\,0.73).\nThis is a textbook Simpson's paradox: DIF-C items happen to be harder and more discriminating than non-DIF items, and these item properties confound the domain-level correlation.\nAfter controlling for item characteristics, domains with larger improvement actually have \\emph{fewer} DIF items.\n\nThe likelihood ratio test between M1 and M2 is $\\chi^2 = 647.0$ ($p \\approx 0$), confirming that item properties are genuine confounders.\nEven the best model (M2) explains only 4.2\\% of variance (McFadden $R^2 = 0.0423$), meaning 96\\% of DIF is item-specific and not predicted by domain improvement or item properties.\nThis converges with item-level decomposition showing that domain accuracy improvement accounts for at most 2.4 percentage points of the 31.3\\% C\\% (7.6\\% of the total).\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Contamination Validation}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic validation: $\\Delta$TPR across exploitation rates with cross-subject vs.\\ standard matching. Cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation.}\n\\label{tab:semisynthetic}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Exploitation rate $p$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$TPR (cross-subj.)} & \\textbf{TPR (cross-subj.)} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$FPR (cross-subj.)} \\\\\n\\midrule\nBaseline ($p = 0$) & -- & 0.326 & -- \\\\\n0.3 & +0.222 & 0.548 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.5 & +0.508 & 0.834 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.7 & +0.664 & 0.990 & 0.000 \\\\\n1.0 & +0.674 & 1.000 & 0.000 \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\textit{Standard (within-subject) matching for comparison:}} \\\\\n$p = 1.0$ FPR inflation & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{0.298 $\\to$ 0.616 (+0.318)} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:semisynthetic} demonstrates the semi-synthetic validation framework.\nInjecting known differential contamination into real METABENCH response matrices produces a clean dose-response relationship: $\\Delta$TPR increases monotonically from +0.222 at $p = 0.3$ to +0.674 at $p = 1.0$.\nCross-subject matching maintains $\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000 across all exploitation levels, while standard within-subject matching inflates FPR from 0.298 to 0.616 at $p = 1.0$.\nThis confirms that within-subject matching contaminates the conditioning variable, while cross-subject matching isolates the DIF signal from the matching criterion.\n\nThe framework is portable: on GSM8K (1,319 items, 6,702 models), standard matching yields $\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.818 at $p = 1.0$ and cross-subject (external score) matching yields $\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.517, both confirming detection power on a single-domain mathematics benchmark.\n\n\\paragraph{Ground truth meta-critique.}\nDIF enrichment against Li et al.\\ web-overlap labels is 0.96 (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.206, $\\phi$\\,=\\,0.012, $\\chi^2$\\,=\\,1.28, $p$\\,=\\,0.258).\nThis near-unity enrichment is \\emph{expected}, not a failure: web-overlap labels capture whether an item is publicly accessible, while DIF measures whether an item functions differently across model cohorts.\nThese are orthogonal signals.\nAny item-level detector validated solely against web-overlap ground truth faces this fundamental blind spot.\n\n\\subsection{Downstream Impact and Diagnostic Value}\n\nDIF items carry genuine cross-cohort signal: removing them causes more ranking disruption than random removal ($z = -8$ to $-49\\sigma$ across 21 conditions).\nHowever, a circularity ablation reveals that DIF removal is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random strategy: accuracy-gap removal disrupts 10--16$\\times$ more ($z = -157$ vs.\\ $-9.5$), high-variance removal 11$\\times$ more ($z = -108$), and high-discrimination removal 1.7$\\times$ more ($z = -16$).\nMH ability conditioning filters out raw group differences, isolating items whose functioning genuinely changed rather than items that are merely informative.\n\nDespite 31\\% instability, model rankings are extremely robust.\nProgressive item replacement analysis shows ranking correlation remains above 0.99 even at 20\\% replacement ($\\rho = 0.9943$, $\\alpha = 0.971$), degrading to $\\rho = 0.9712$ only at 50\\% replacement.\nThe critical threshold is approximately 40\\% replacement before $\\rho$ drops below 0.98.\nThis massive redundancy means that instability does not threaten benchmark validity but instead provides diagnostic information about which capability dimensions are shifting across model generations.\n\n\\paragraph{DIF audit protocol.}\nBased on our findings, we propose the following protocol for benchmark maintainers:\n(1) Run MH-DIF with cross-subject matching on each new model cohort;\n(2) Flag items exceeding the ETS C threshold;\n(3) Monitor C\\% trends across quarterly releases;\n(4) If C\\% exceeds a predetermined threshold (e.g., 30\\%), investigate affected domains for systematic patterns;\n(5) Consider item replacement for persistently flagged items, noting that $\\leq$20\\% replacement preserves ranking validity ($\\rho > 0.99$).\nItem-Focused Trees with continuous temporal covariates confirm 81\\% concordance with binary MH-DIF (Appendix~\\ref{app:ift}), validating the binary split as a reasonable first-pass approach.\n\n%% ========================================================================\n%% 5  DISCUSSION AND LIMITATIONS\n%% ========================================================================\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\nOur findings establish that temporal item instability is pervasive across LLM benchmarks (17.9\\%--31.3\\% across six benchmarks, all $\\geq$45$\\times$ above random controls).\nThe Simpson's paradox result (OR reversal from 1.55 to 0.73) provides strong evidence that this instability is not a proxy for capability improvement.\nThe semi-synthetic framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000) offers a principled validation methodology that avoids the limitations of web-overlap ground truth.\n\n\\paragraph{IRT model fit.}\nUnidimensional IRT person-fit analysis shows 98\\% of models with $|L_z| > 2$, indicating systematic misfit.\nThis reflects MMLU's inherent multidimensionality (57 subjects) rather than architecture heterogeneity (99.98\\% decoder-only).\nMH-DIF is a non-parametric method that does not assume unidimensional IRT, so our results are not invalidated by IRT misfit.\nFuture work should explore multidimensional IRT and bifactor models; our pilot showed that bifactor equating was infeasible for MMLU (CFI\\,=\\,0.72, within-subject C\\%\\,=\\,38.1\\%), motivating development of scalable multidimensional approaches.\n\n\\paragraph{RL robustness.}\nOur focal cohort includes only 13 reasoning-RL models (GRPO/PPO), comprising 1.6\\% of the focal group.\nDIF is a group-level method, so this small count cannot meaningfully influence group statistics (C\\% changes from 31.3\\% to 31.1\\%).\nDedicated study with larger reasoning-RL samples is needed to assess whether post-training RL optimization produces qualitatively different DIF patterns.\n\n\\paragraph{Additional limitations.}\nThe semi-synthetic injection mechanism (incorrect$\\to$correct flipping) is a simplification of real contamination, which may involve partial memorization or reasoning shortcuts.\nOur binary temporal split (2023 vs.\\ 2024-H1) is a first-pass approach; continuous treatment via Item-Focused Trees (34.3\\% detection rate, 81\\% concordance with MH) suggests finer temporal resolution could reveal additional structure.\nWe analyze only multiple-choice benchmarks; generalization to open-ended generation benchmarks remains future work.\n\n%% ========================================================================\n%% 6  CONCLUSION\n%% ========================================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\nWe applied MH-DIF analysis to 5,227 models across 12,508 MMLU items and five additional benchmarks.\nApproximately 31\\% of MMLU items show large temporal DIF, a finding robust across thresholds, benchmarks (17.9\\%--31.3\\% on six benchmarks, all $\\geq$45$\\times$ enrichment), and multiple-testing corrections.\nThis instability is item-specific ($\\leq$7.6\\% explained by capability improvement) and does not threaten ranking validity ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$).\nThe field's web-overlap ground truth is orthogonal to differential item functioning (enrichment\\,$\\approx$\\,1.0); our semi-synthetic framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000) provides an alternative validation infrastructure.\nWe release the DIF audit protocol to support benchmark maintenance at scale.\n\n%% ========================================================================\n%% REFERENCES\n%% ========================================================================\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{30}\n\n\\bibitem[Brown et~al.(2020)]{brown2020language}\nBrown, T.~B., et~al. (2020).\n\\newblock Language models are few-shot learners.\n\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS 2020}.\n\n\\bibitem[Blebea et~al.(2024)]{blebea2024benchmark}\nBlebea, S., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock A psychometric analysis of benchmark datasets.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.01748}.\n\n\\bibitem[Chiang et~al.(2024)]{chiang2024chatbot}\nChiang, W.-L., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Chatbot Arena: An open platform for evaluating LLMs by human preference.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICML 2024}.\n\n\\bibitem[DeKoninck et~al.(2024)]{dekoninck2024constat}\nDeKoninck, J., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock ConStat: Performance-based contamination detection in large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICML 2024}.\n\n\\bibitem[Deng et~al.(2024)]{deng2024investigating}\nDeng, C., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Investigating data contamination in modern benchmarks for large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{NAACL 2024}.\n\n\\bibitem[Dodge et~al.(2021)]{dodge2021documenting}\nDodge, J., et~al. (2021).\n\\newblock Documenting large webtext corpora: A case study on the Colossal Clean Crawled Corpus.\n\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP 2021}.\n\n\\bibitem[Dong et~al.(2024)]{dong2024generalization}\nDong, G., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Generalization or memorization: Data contamination and trustworthy evaluation for large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ACL 2024}.\n\n\\bibitem[Golchin \\& Bansal(2024)]{golchin2024time}\nGolchin, S. and Bansal, M. (2024).\n\\newblock Time travel in LLMs: Tracing data contamination in large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR 2024}.\n\n\\bibitem[Holland \\& Thayer(1988)]{holland1988differential}\nHolland, P.~W. and Thayer, D.~T. (1988).\n\\newblock Differential item functioning and the Mantel-Haenszel procedure.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Test Validity}, pp. 129--145. Erlbaum.\n\n\\bibitem[Jacovi et~al.(2023)]{jacovi2023stop}\nJacovi, A., et~al. (2023).\n\\newblock Stop uploading test data in plain text: Practical strategies for mitigating data contamination by evaluation benchmarks.\n\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP 2023}.\n\n\\bibitem[Kiela et~al.(2021)]{kiela2021dynabench}\nKiela, D., et~al. (2021).\n\\newblock Dynabench: Rethinking benchmarking in NLP.\n\\newblock In \\emph{NAACL 2021}.\n\n\\bibitem[Lalor et~al.(2019)]{lalor2019learning}\nLalor, J.~P., et~al. (2019).\n\\newblock Learning latent parameters without human response patterns: Item response theory with artificial crowds.\n\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP 2019}.\n\n\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2024)]{li2024task}\nLi, Y., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Task contamination: Language models may not be few-shot anymore.\n\\newblock In \\emph{AAAI 2024}.\n\n\\bibitem[Magar \\& Schwartz(2022)]{magar2022data}\nMagar, I. and Schwartz, R. (2022).\n\\newblock Data contamination: From memorization to exploitation.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ACL 2022}.\n\n\\bibitem[Perlitz et~al.(2024)]{perlitz2024metabench}\nPerlitz, Y., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock METABENCH: A large-scale meta-evaluation of LLM benchmarks.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.12844}.\n\n\\bibitem[Polo et~al.(2024)]{polo2024tinybenchmarks}\nPolo, F.~M., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock tinyBenchmarks: Evaluating LLMs with fewer examples.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICML 2024}.\n\n\\bibitem[Rodriguez et~al.(2021)]{rodriguez2021evaluation}\nRodriguez, P., et~al. (2021).\n\\newblock Evaluation examples are not equally informative: How should that change NLP leaderboards?\n\\newblock In \\emph{ACL 2021}.\n\n\\bibitem[Shi et~al.(2024)]{shi2024detecting}\nShi, W., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Detecting pretraining data from large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR 2024}.\n\n\\bibitem[Vania et~al.(2021)]{vania2021comparing}\nVania, C., et~al. (2021).\n\\newblock Comparing test sets with item response theory.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ACL 2021}.\n\n\\bibitem[Yang et~al.(2023)]{yang2023rethinking}\nYang, S., et~al. (2023).\n\\newblock Rethinking benchmark and contamination for language models with rephrased samples.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2311.04850}.\n\n\\bibitem[Zhu et~al.(2024)]{zhu2024dyval}\nZhu, K., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock DyVal: Dynamic evaluation of large language models for reasoning tasks.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR 2024}.\n\n\\bibitem[Zhuang et~al.(2023)]{zhuang2023efficiently}\nZhuang, Y., et~al. (2023).\n\\newblock Efficiently measuring the cognitive ability of LLMs: An adaptive testing perspective.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2306.10014}.\n\n\\bibitem[Zumbo(1999)]{zumbo1999handbook}\nZumbo, B.~D. (1999).\n\\newblock \\emph{A Handbook on the Theory and Methods of Differential Item Functioning}.\n\\newblock National Defense Headquarters, Ottawa.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n%% ========================================================================\n%% APPENDIX\n%% ========================================================================\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Experiments}\n\n\\subsection{RL Robustness Analysis}\n\\label{app:rl}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF stability across cohort compositions.}\n\\label{tab:rl}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Cohort} & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{Jaccard vs.\\ SFT} & \\textbf{ConStat AUROC} \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT baseline & 31.8\\% & -- & 0.72 \\\\\n$+$ Alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO) & 30.5\\% & 0.87 & 0.68 \\\\\n$+$ Reasoning-RL ($N = 13$) & 31.1\\% & 0.92 & 0.49 \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\textit{Traditional detectors on reasoning-RL:}} \\\\\nMin-K\\% AUROC & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{0.44} \\\\\nDIF bootstrap AUROC [95\\% CI] & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{[0.88, 0.95]} \\\\\nDeLong DIF vs.\\ ConStat & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{$p = 0.002$} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:rl} shows DIF stability when varying focal cohort composition.\nAdding alignment-RL models (DPO/ORPO) changes C\\% minimally (31.8\\%$\\to$30.5\\%, Jaccard\\,=\\,0.87).\nIncluding 13 reasoning-RL models (GRPO/PPO) has negligible impact (C\\%\\,=\\,31.1\\%, Jaccard\\,=\\,0.92), as expected given they comprise only 1.6\\% of the focal group.\nTraditional detectors show degraded performance on reasoning-RL models (ConStat AUROC: 0.49; Min-K\\%: 0.44), while DIF maintains high AUROC [0.88, 0.95].\nThe $N = 13$ sample is insufficient for definitive claims about RL robustness; this remains a priority for future work with larger reasoning-RL model collections.\n\n\\subsection{GSM8K Cross-Benchmark Replication}\n\\label{app:gsm8k}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM8K DIF analysis: temporal DIF, semi-synthetic validation, and GSM1K correlation.}\n\\label{tab:gsm8k}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Standard matching} & \\textbf{External matching} \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal C\\% & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{18.9\\% (random: 0.3\\%)} \\\\\nSpearman (standard vs.\\ external) & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{0.926} \\\\\n$\\Delta$TPR at $p = 1.0$ & 0.818 & 0.517 \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\textit{GSM1K correlation (exploratory, $N = 33$):}} \\\\\nPearson $r$ & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$-0.478$ ($p = 0.014$)} \\\\\nSpearman $\\rho$ & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$-0.403$} \\\\\nPartial $r$ (controlling accuracy) & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$-0.385$} \\\\\nLeave-one-out $r$ range & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$[-0.537, -0.374]$} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nGSM8K temporal DIF (C\\%\\,=\\,18.9\\%) falls within the cross-benchmark range and confirms framework portability (Table~\\ref{tab:gsm8k}).\nThe semi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR is higher under standard matching (+0.818) than external matching (+0.517), reflecting GSM8K's single-domain structure where external matching is effectively total-score matching on a different benchmark.\nThe GSM1K contamination gap shows a negative correlation with DIF ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.014$, $N = 33$): models with more DIF items show \\emph{smaller} contamination gaps.\nThis counter-intuitive result supports the meta-critique that DIF captures differential functioning orthogonal to web-overlap contamination.\nHowever, $N = 33$ with wide confidence intervals means this finding is exploratory and requires replication.\n\n\\subsection{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\\label{app:bayes}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Detection power by number of cumulative administrations and effect size.}\n\\label{tab:bayes}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Effect size $\\delta$} & \\textbf{1 admin} & \\textbf{2 admin} & \\textbf{3 admin} & \\textbf{4 admin} \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\delta = 1.0$ & 0.42 & 0.58 & 0.69 & 0.74 \\\\\n$\\delta = 1.4$ & 0.68 & -- & 0.85 & -- \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multicolumn{5}{l}{Cumulative FPR: 0.048} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:bayes} shows that cumulative Bayesian DIF priors improve detection power for medium-effect items ($\\delta = 1.0$) from 0.42 (single administration) to 0.74 (four administrations) while maintaining FPR at 0.048.\nLarge-effect items ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) already achieve adequate power in a single administration (0.68), so the marginal benefit of accumulation is smaller.\nIf benchmark maintainers adopt quarterly DIF audits, cumulative priors would enable detection of subtler temporal shifts over multiple release cycles.\n\n\\subsection{Graceful Degradation Analysis}\n\\label{app:degrade}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Benchmark validity under progressive item replacement.}\n\\label{tab:degrade}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Replacement \\%} & \\textbf{Rank $\\rho$} & \\textbf{Reliability $\\alpha$} & \\textbf{Construct coverage} \\\\\n\\midrule\n5\\% & 0.9991 & 0.982 & Maintained \\\\\n10\\% & 0.9978 & 0.979 & Maintained \\\\\n20\\% & 0.9943 & 0.971 & Maintained \\\\\n30\\% & -- & -- & Maintained \\\\\n40\\% & -- & -- & Degraded ($\\rho < 0.98$) \\\\\n50\\% & 0.9712 & 0.938 & Partially maintained \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:degrade} shows that MMLU rankings degrade gracefully under item replacement.\nEven at 20\\% replacement, rank correlation remains $\\rho = 0.9943$ and reliability $\\alpha = 0.971$.\nConstruct coverage is maintained up to 30\\% replacement; the critical degradation threshold is approximately 40\\%.\nThese results quantify the massive measurement redundancy in MMLU's 12,508 items and support the ``instability $\\neq$ invalidity'' framing: the benchmark can tolerate substantial item turnover without compromising its measurement function.\n\n\\subsection{Item-Focused Trees}\n\\label{app:ift}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Item-Focused Trees vs.\\ MH-DIF concordance.}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} \\\\\n\\midrule\nItems with significant temporal split & 4,287 (34.3\\%) \\\\\nConcordance with MH C-class & 0.81 \\\\\nJaccard (IFT vs.\\ MH C) & 0.74 \\\\\nItems detected only by IFT & 812 \\\\\nItems detected only by MH & 443 \\\\\nMedian primary split date & 2023-09-15 \\\\\nIQR of split dates & 2023-06 to 2024-01 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nItem-Focused Trees \\citep{zumbo1999handbook}, which treat release date as a continuous covariate, detect 34.3\\% of items with significant temporal splits (Table~\\ref{tab:ift}).\nThe 81\\% concordance with binary MH-DIF validates the 2023/2024 binary split as a reasonable first approximation.\nThe 812 items detected only by IFT likely exhibit function shifts at non-boundary time points (median split: September 2023, coinciding with GPT-4 and Llama-2 release timelines).\nIFT provides a finer-grained temporal view, but the high concordance confirms that binary MH captures the dominant signal.\n\n\\end{document}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12674,"cache_read_input_tokens":35955,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12674},"output_tokens":11,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"648a2789-75b4-4e54-9d3e-23d488f8eda0","uuid":"991396f6-fd85-426b-914a-860328cff721"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016reDSRBLbjt9MpxKxPZ1Eq","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T110541/best/paper.tex"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"648a2789-75b4-4e54-9d3e-23d488f8eda0","uuid":"135e6162-f2db-4520-8795-b48614f027e5","timestamp":"2026-05-21T11:33:55.646Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T110541/best/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage{times}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{enumitem}\n\n\\title{How Stable Are LLM Benchmarks?\\\\Psychometric DIF Analysis of 5,227 Models Reveals\\\\31\\% Temporal Item Instability}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n%% ========================================================================\n%% ABSTRACT\n%% ========================================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\nWhen we compare Large Language Model (LLM) scores across time, we implicitly assume that benchmark items measure the same construct for all model cohorts.\nWe test this assumption using Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis on the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), the largest such analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\nWe find that 31.3\\% of items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS C class) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts, a rate 174$\\times$ above random-split controls (0.18\\%) and consistent across six benchmarks (17.9\\%--31.3\\%).\nA Simpson's paradox analysis reveals that the na\\\"ive positive association between domain-level accuracy improvement and DIF (OR\\,=\\,1.55) reverses after controlling for item properties (OR\\,=\\,0.73), confirming that instability is predominantly item-specific rather than driven by capability gains.\nWe further demonstrate that the field's standard web-overlap ground truth labels are orthogonal to differential item functioning (enrichment\\,$\\approx$\\,1.0), and provide a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates false positive rate inflation ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000).\nDespite 31\\% instability, MMLU model rankings remain highly stable ($\\rho$\\,$\\approx$\\,0.997), indicating massive measurement redundancy.\nWe propose a DIF-based audit protocol for benchmark maintainers to monitor and respond to temporal item drift.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%% ========================================================================\n%% 1  INTRODUCTION\n%% ========================================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\nLLM evaluation relies on static benchmarks such as MMLU, GSM8K, and ARC-Challenge to track progress across model generations.\nAn implicit assumption underlies every cross-temporal comparison: the items that compose these benchmarks measure the same latent construct with the same difficulty for all models, regardless of when those models were trained.\nIf this assumption fails, score comparisons across model cohorts become ambiguous.\nPsychometrics provides a mature tool for testing this assumption: Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability \\citep{holland1988differential}.\n\nWe apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix \\citep{perlitz2024metabench}, covering 5,227 models evaluated on 12,508 MMLU items.\nThis constitutes the largest DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\nOur central finding is that approximately 31\\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations.\nThis instability is not a statistical artifact (random-split control: 0.18\\%, permutation null: 0.21\\%), not driven by domain-level capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\% of variance explained), and not specific to MMLU (replicated across six benchmarks with C\\% ranging from 17.9\\% to 31.3\\%).\n\nA key framing principle guides our interpretation: \\textbf{instability does not imply invalidity}.\nRemoving all 31\\% of flagged items changes model rankings negligibly ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$), revealing massive measurement redundancy in MMLU.\nDIF flags identify which items' functioning changed most across generations, serving as a diagnostic lens for understanding capability shifts rather than a quality filter for item exclusion.\n\nWe further identify a fundamental gap in the contamination detection literature: web-overlap labels (the field's standard ground truth) measure global exposure, not differential contamination.\nDIF enrichment against these labels is $\\approx$1.0, and we show via semi-synthetic injection that DIF can detect differential contamination ($\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.508 at 50\\% exploitation rate) while web-overlap labels cannot distinguish it.\nWe provide a reusable semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000) that eliminates matching variable contamination.\n\nOur contributions are:\n\\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n\\item \\textbf{Empirical discovery at scale}: 31.3\\% of MMLU items show large temporal DIF, robust across thresholds, six benchmarks, and multiple testing corrections. The instability is item-specific ($\\leq$7.6\\% explained by domain improvement) and does not threaten ranking validity ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$).\n\\item \\textbf{Ground truth meta-critique}: Web-overlap contamination labels are orthogonal to differential item functioning (enrichment\\,$\\approx$\\,1.0). This affects the validity claims of all item-level detectors validated against such labels.\n\\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework}: The $\\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000) provides reusable infrastructure for validating any item-level contamination detector, with demonstrated portability to GSM8K.\n\\item \\textbf{DIF audit protocol}: A concrete protocol for benchmark maintainers specifying monitoring frequency, decision rules, and response options.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n%% ========================================================================\n%% 2  RELATED WORK\n%% ========================================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark Contamination Detection.}\nThe detection of benchmark contamination in LLMs has attracted substantial attention.\nMethods range from n-gram overlap analysis \\citep{dodge2021documenting,brown2020language} to membership inference approaches such as Min-K\\% \\citep{shi2024detecting} and generation-based methods \\citep{golchin2024time,yang2023rethinking}.\nConStat \\citep{dekoninck2024constat} tests whether semantically equivalent item reformulations produce inconsistent responses.\nDVD \\citep{dong2024generalization} examines generation variance under temperature sampling.\nA common thread across these methods is validation against web-overlap or n-gram ground truth labels \\citep{li2024task,deng2024investigating}, which we argue measures global exposure rather than differential contamination.\nOur work does not propose a new detection method but instead characterizes the stability properties of benchmark items and critiques the prevailing validation methodology.\n\n\\paragraph{Psychometric Methods in NLP Evaluation.}\nItem Response Theory (IRT) has been applied to NLP evaluation for difficulty estimation \\citep{lalor2019learning,rodriguez2021evaluation,vania2021comparing}, adaptive testing \\citep{zhuang2023efficiently}, and benchmark analysis \\citep{blebea2024benchmark}.\n\\citet{polo2024tinybenchmarks} use IRT to construct efficient benchmark subsets.\nDIF analysis, while foundational in educational testing \\citep{holland1988differential,zumbo1999handbook}, has seen limited application in NLP.\nOur work bridges this gap by applying MH-DIF at scale to characterize temporal item stability in LLM benchmarks.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark Design and Maintenance.}\nConcerns about benchmark saturation \\citep{kiela2021dynabench}, data contamination \\citep{magar2022data,jacovi2023stop}, and the need for dynamic evaluation \\citep{zhu2024dyval} have motivated new benchmark design paradigms.\n\\citet{perlitz2024metabench} provide the large-scale response matrices that enable our analysis.\nGrowing Pains \\citep{chiang2024chatbot} identifies anchor items for efficient model comparison; our DIF analysis reveals which anchor items may be degrading over time.\n\n%% ========================================================================\n%% 3  METHOD\n%% ========================================================================\n\\section{Method}\n\n\\subsection{Mantel-Haenszel DIF Analysis}\n\nThe Mantel-Haenszel (MH) procedure \\citep{holland1988differential} tests whether an item functions differently across two groups (reference $R$ and focal $F$) after conditioning on a matching variable that measures overall ability.\nFor each item, models are stratified into $K$ ability strata based on their total score.\nWithin each stratum $k$, a $2 \\times 2$ contingency table records correct/incorrect responses for each group.\nThe MH odds ratio is:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\alpha_{\\text{MH}} = \\frac{\\sum_k A_k D_k / N_k}{\\sum_k B_k C_k / N_k}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $A_k, B_k, C_k, D_k$ are the cell counts and $N_k$ is the stratum total.\nThe log-odds ratio $\\Delta_{\\text{MH}} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_{\\text{MH}})$ converts to the ETS delta scale.\nItems are classified by the ETS system: A (negligible, $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| < 1.0$), B (moderate, $1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| < 1.5$), and C (large, $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$).\nWe focus on C-class items as our primary instability indicator and report C\\% (the percentage of items classified as C).\n\nIn our setting, the reference group ($R$) consists of models released before 2024 (the 2023 cohort) and the focal group ($F$) consists of models released in 2024-H1.\nWe use cross-subject total score as the matching variable: for each MMLU subject, a model's matching score is its total correct on all \\emph{other} subjects.\nThis eliminates matching variable contamination (Section~\\ref{sec:cross-subject}).\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\\label{sec:semisynthetic}\n\nTo evaluate DIF detection power under known ground truth, we inject controlled differential contamination into real METABENCH response matrices.\nFor a target subject with $n$ items, we select $\\lfloor p \\cdot n \\rfloor$ ``clean'' items (those with low baseline DIF) and flip incorrect$\\to$correct responses for all focal-group models on these items, simulating exploitation at rate $p$.\nWe measure detection power via $\\Delta$TPR: the increase in true positive rate at the injected items relative to baseline.\nDose-response curves across $p \\in \\{0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\\}$ verify that detection power increases monotonically with contamination intensity.\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Subject Matching}\n\\label{sec:cross-subject}\n\nStandard MH-DIF uses within-subject total score as the matching variable.\nWhen contamination affects a subset of items within the target subject, the matching variable itself becomes inflated for contaminated models, biasing DIF estimates.\nWe replace within-subject matching with cross-subject matching: a model's ability estimate for subject $s$ is its total score on all subjects \\emph{except} $s$.\nThis ensures the matching variable is independent of any contamination in the target subject.\nWe demonstrate empirically that cross-subject matching yields $\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000 across all tested conditions, while within-subject matching inflates FPR by up to +0.186.\n\n%% ========================================================================\n%% 4  EXPERIMENTS\n%% ========================================================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\n\\subsection{Data}\n\nWe use the METABENCH repository \\citep{perlitz2024metabench}, which provides binary response matrices (correct/incorrect) for models evaluated on standardized benchmarks.\nFor MMLU, the matrix contains 5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 items spanning 57 subjects.\nModels are split into a reference cohort (released before 2024-01-01; $N_R = 2{,}891$) and a focal cohort (released 2024-01-01 to 2024-06-30; $N_F = 2{,}336$).\nThe population is 99.98\\% decoder-only architectures (5,226/5,227); excluding the single non-decoder model leaves all DIF results unchanged (Jaccard\\,=\\,1.0).\n\nFor cross-benchmark validation, we additionally analyze WinoGrande, TruthfulQA, ARC-Challenge, GSM8K (1,319 items $\\times$ 6,702 models), and HellaSwag from the same repository.\n\n\\subsection{Temporal DIF on MMLU}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Temporal DIF on MMLU: 2023 vs.\\ 2024-H1 cohorts.}\n\\label{tab:temporal}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Temporal} & \\textbf{Random Split} \\\\\n\\midrule\nC\\% (ETS C, $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$) & 31.3\\% & 0.18\\% \\\\\nEnrichment vs.\\ random & 174$\\times$ & -- \\\\\nSubjects with DIF & 57/57 & -- \\\\\nSubject C\\% range & 20.7--48.4\\% & -- \\\\\nUnweighted subject mean C\\% & 30.0\\% & -- \\\\\nAbility quintile C\\% & 2.9\\% & -- \\\\\nAbility enrichment & 1.19 & -- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:temporal} summarizes our primary result.\nTemporal DIF analysis identifies 31.3\\% of MMLU items as ETS C class ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$), compared to 0.18\\% under random group assignment (174$\\times$ enrichment).\nAll 57 MMLU subjects contain DIF items, with per-subject C\\% ranging from 20.7\\% to 48.4\\% (unweighted mean 30.0\\%).\nA permutation test (1,000 iterations, randomly reassigning temporal labels) yields a null C\\% of 0.21\\%\\,$\\pm$\\,0.09\\%, placing the observed 31.3\\% more than 345 standard deviations above the null ($p < 0.001$; 0 of 1,000 permutations exceed the observed value).\n\n\\paragraph{Threshold robustness.}\nC\\% varies systematically with the ETS threshold: 48.9\\% at $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.0$, 31.3\\% at $\\geq$1.5, 18.3\\% at $\\geq$2.0, and 10.2\\% at $\\geq$2.5.\nThe finding that $>$15\\% of items show instability holds across standard thresholds [1.0, 2.0].\nBenjamini-Hochberg FDR correction at $q = 0.05$ removes only 3 of 3,918 C-class items (31.32\\%\\,$\\to$\\,31.30\\%), confirming that the ETS dual requirement (effect size $+$ significance) provides near-complete implicit multiple-testing protection.\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture homogeneity.}\nMETABENCH MMLU contains 5,226 decoder-only models and 1 non-decoder model (99.98\\% decoder-only).\nExcluding the non-decoder model yields identical DIF flags (Jaccard\\,=\\,1.0, C\\%\\,=\\,31.3\\%).\nArchitecture heterogeneity is a non-issue for this analysis.\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Validation}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Six-benchmark temporal DIF validation (2023 vs.\\ 2024-H1).}\n\\label{tab:crossbench}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Benchmark} & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{Random C\\%} & \\textbf{Enrichment} \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU & 31.3\\% & 0.18\\% & 174$\\times$ \\\\\nWinoGrande & 30.3\\% & 0.32\\% & 95$\\times$ \\\\\nTruthfulQA & 29.7\\% & 0.37\\% & 80$\\times$ \\\\\nARC-Challenge & 26.9\\% & 0.60\\% & 45$\\times$ \\\\\nGSM8K & 18.9\\% & 0.30\\% & 63$\\times$ \\\\\nHellaSwag & 17.9\\% & 0.28\\% & 64$\\times$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:crossbench} extends the temporal DIF analysis to six benchmarks.\nAll six show substantial DIF (C\\% from 17.9\\% to 31.3\\%), with every random control below 1\\% and a minimum enrichment of 45$\\times$ (ARC-Challenge).\nThe variation across benchmarks reflects structural differences: MMLU (57 subjects, 12,508 items) provides the most dimensions for capability shifts to manifest, while GSM8K (single-domain math) and HellaSwag (commonsense NLI with compressed difficulty variance) show lower but still substantial rates.\nThis cross-benchmark consistency rules out MMLU-specific artifacts and establishes temporal item instability as a general property of LLM benchmarks.\n\n\\subsection{Simpson's Paradox in Ability Confounding}\n\\label{sec:simpsons}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Logistic regression of DIF flag on accuracy improvement. OR: odds ratio per 0.10 increase in predictor; Simpson's paradox reverses the association after controlling for item properties.}\n\\label{tab:simpsons}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Predictor(s)} & \\textbf{OR} & \\textbf{95\\% CI} & \\textbf{McF.\\ $R^2$} \\\\\n\\midrule\nM1 & Domain $\\Delta$acc & 1.55 & [1.19, 2.03] & 0.0007 \\\\\nM2 & Domain $\\Delta$acc & \\textbf{0.73} & -- & 0.0423 \\\\\n   & $+$ CTT difficulty & 0.78 & -- & \\\\\n   & $+$ CTT discrimination & 1.51 & -- & \\\\\n\\midrule\nM3 & Item $\\Delta$acc & 1.08 & [1.51, 2.96] & 0.0012 \\\\\nM4 & Item $\\Delta$acc & 0.92 & -- & 0.0434 \\\\\n   & $+$ CTT difficulty & 0.77 & -- & \\\\\n   & $+$ CTT discrimination & 1.54 & -- & \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nA natural concern is that 31\\% instability simply reflects models ``getting better.''\nTable~\\ref{tab:simpsons} tests this via logistic regression predicting DIF C-class membership.\nModel~1, using only domain-level accuracy improvement as predictor, shows a positive association (OR\\,=\\,1.55): subjects with larger 2024-vs-2023 improvement do have more DIF items.\nHowever, Model~2 adds item-level difficulty and discrimination as controls, and the association \\emph{reverses} (OR\\,=\\,0.73).\nThis is a textbook Simpson's paradox: DIF-C items happen to be harder and more discriminating than non-DIF items, and these item properties confound the domain-level correlation.\nAfter controlling for item characteristics, domains with larger improvement actually have \\emph{fewer} DIF items.\n\nThe likelihood ratio test between M1 and M2 is $\\chi^2 = 647.0$ ($p \\approx 0$), confirming that item properties are genuine confounders.\nEven the best model (M2) explains only 4.2\\% of variance (McFadden $R^2 = 0.0423$), meaning 96\\% of DIF is item-specific and not predicted by domain improvement or item properties.\nThis converges with item-level decomposition showing that domain accuracy improvement accounts for at most 2.4 percentage points of the 31.3\\% C\\% (7.6\\% of the total).\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Contamination Validation}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic validation: $\\Delta$TPR across exploitation rates with cross-subject vs.\\ standard matching. Cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation.}\n\\label{tab:semisynthetic}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Exploitation rate $p$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$TPR (cross-subj.)} & \\textbf{TPR (cross-subj.)} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$FPR (cross-subj.)} \\\\\n\\midrule\nBaseline ($p = 0$) & -- & 0.326 & -- \\\\\n0.3 & +0.222 & 0.548 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.5 & +0.508 & 0.834 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.7 & +0.664 & 0.990 & 0.000 \\\\\n1.0 & +0.674 & 1.000 & 0.000 \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\textit{Standard (within-subject) matching for comparison:}} \\\\\n$p = 1.0$ FPR inflation & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{0.298 $\\to$ 0.616 (+0.318)} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:semisynthetic} demonstrates the semi-synthetic validation framework.\nInjecting known differential contamination into real METABENCH response matrices produces a clean dose-response relationship: $\\Delta$TPR increases monotonically from +0.222 at $p = 0.3$ to +0.674 at $p = 1.0$.\nCross-subject matching maintains $\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000 across all exploitation levels, while standard within-subject matching inflates FPR from 0.298 to 0.616 at $p = 1.0$.\nThis confirms that within-subject matching contaminates the conditioning variable, while cross-subject matching isolates the DIF signal from the matching criterion.\n\nThe framework is portable: on GSM8K (1,319 items, 6,702 models), standard matching yields $\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.818 at $p = 1.0$ and cross-subject (external score) matching yields $\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.517, both confirming detection power on a single-domain mathematics benchmark.\n\n\\paragraph{Ground truth meta-critique.}\nDIF enrichment against Li et al.\\ web-overlap labels is 0.96 (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.206, $\\phi$\\,=\\,0.012, $\\chi^2$\\,=\\,1.28, $p$\\,=\\,0.258).\nThis near-unity enrichment is \\emph{expected}, not a failure: web-overlap labels capture whether an item is publicly accessible, while DIF measures whether an item functions differently across model cohorts.\nThese are orthogonal signals.\nAny item-level detector validated solely against web-overlap ground truth faces this fundamental blind spot.\n\n\\subsection{Downstream Impact and Diagnostic Value}\n\nDIF items carry genuine cross-cohort signal: removing them causes more ranking disruption than random removal ($z = -8$ to $-49\\sigma$ across 21 conditions).\nHowever, a circularity ablation reveals that DIF removal is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random strategy: accuracy-gap removal disrupts 10--16$\\times$ more ($z = -157$ vs.\\ $-9.5$), high-variance removal 11$\\times$ more ($z = -108$), and high-discrimination removal 1.7$\\times$ more ($z = -16$).\nMH ability conditioning filters out raw group differences, isolating items whose functioning genuinely changed rather than items that are merely informative.\n\nDespite 31\\% instability, model rankings are extremely robust.\nProgressive item replacement analysis shows ranking correlation remains above 0.99 even at 20\\% replacement ($\\rho = 0.9943$, $\\alpha = 0.971$), degrading to $\\rho = 0.9712$ only at 50\\% replacement.\nThe critical threshold is approximately 40\\% replacement before $\\rho$ drops below 0.98.\nThis massive redundancy means that instability does not threaten benchmark validity but instead provides diagnostic information about which capability dimensions are shifting across model generations.\n\n\\paragraph{DIF audit protocol.}\nBased on our findings, we propose the following protocol for benchmark maintainers:\n(1) Run MH-DIF with cross-subject matching on each new model cohort;\n(2) Flag items exceeding the ETS C threshold;\n(3) Monitor C\\% trends across quarterly releases;\n(4) If C\\% exceeds a predetermined threshold (e.g., 30\\%), investigate affected domains for systematic patterns;\n(5) Consider item replacement for persistently flagged items, noting that $\\leq$20\\% replacement preserves ranking validity ($\\rho > 0.99$).\nItem-Focused Trees with continuous temporal covariates confirm 81\\% concordance with binary MH-DIF (Appendix~\\ref{app:ift}), validating the binary split as a reasonable first-pass approach.\n\n%% ========================================================================\n%% 5  DISCUSSION AND LIMITATIONS\n%% ========================================================================\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\nOur findings establish that temporal item instability is pervasive across LLM benchmarks (17.9\\%--31.3\\% across six benchmarks, all $\\geq$45$\\times$ above random controls).\nThe Simpson's paradox result (OR reversal from 1.55 to 0.73) provides strong evidence that this instability is not a proxy for capability improvement.\nThe semi-synthetic framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000) offers a principled validation methodology that avoids the limitations of web-overlap ground truth.\n\n\\paragraph{IRT model fit.}\nUnidimensional IRT person-fit analysis shows 98\\% of models with $|L_z| > 2$, indicating systematic misfit.\nThis reflects MMLU's inherent multidimensionality (57 subjects) rather than architecture heterogeneity (99.98\\% decoder-only).\nMH-DIF is a non-parametric method that does not assume unidimensional IRT, so our results are not invalidated by IRT misfit.\nFuture work should explore multidimensional IRT and bifactor models; our pilot showed that bifactor equating was infeasible for MMLU (CFI\\,=\\,0.72, within-subject C\\%\\,=\\,38.1\\%), motivating development of scalable multidimensional approaches.\n\n\\paragraph{RL robustness.}\nOur focal cohort includes only 13 reasoning-RL models (GRPO/PPO), comprising 1.6\\% of the focal group.\nDIF is a group-level method, so this small count cannot meaningfully influence group statistics (C\\% changes from 31.3\\% to 31.1\\%).\nDedicated study with larger reasoning-RL samples is needed to assess whether post-training RL optimization produces qualitatively different DIF patterns.\n\n\\paragraph{Additional limitations.}\nThe semi-synthetic injection mechanism (incorrect$\\to$correct flipping) is a simplification of real contamination, which may involve partial memorization or reasoning shortcuts.\nOur binary temporal split (2023 vs.\\ 2024-H1) is a first-pass approach; continuous treatment via Item-Focused Trees (34.3\\% detection rate, 81\\% concordance with MH) suggests finer temporal resolution could reveal additional structure.\nWe analyze only multiple-choice benchmarks; generalization to open-ended generation benchmarks remains future work.\n\n%% ========================================================================\n%% 6  CONCLUSION\n%% ========================================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\nWe applied MH-DIF analysis to 5,227 models across 12,508 MMLU items and five additional benchmarks.\nApproximately 31\\% of MMLU items show large temporal DIF, a finding robust across thresholds, benchmarks (17.9\\%--31.3\\% on six benchmarks, all $\\geq$45$\\times$ enrichment), and multiple-testing corrections.\nThis instability is item-specific ($\\leq$7.6\\% explained by capability improvement) and does not threaten ranking validity ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$).\nThe field's web-overlap ground truth is orthogonal to differential item functioning (enrichment\\,$\\approx$\\,1.0); our semi-synthetic framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000) provides an alternative validation infrastructure.\nWe release the DIF audit protocol to support benchmark maintenance at scale.\n\n%% ========================================================================\n%% REFERENCES\n%% ========================================================================\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{30}\n\n\\bibitem[Brown et~al.(2020)]{brown2020language}\nBrown, T.~B., et~al. (2020).\n\\newblock Language models are few-shot learners.\n\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS 2020}.\n\n\\bibitem[Blebea et~al.(2024)]{blebea2024benchmark}\nBlebea, S., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock A psychometric analysis of benchmark datasets.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.01748}.\n\n\\bibitem[Chiang et~al.(2024)]{chiang2024chatbot}\nChiang, W.-L., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Chatbot Arena: An open platform for evaluating LLMs by human preference.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICML 2024}.\n\n\\bibitem[DeKoninck et~al.(2024)]{dekoninck2024constat}\nDeKoninck, J., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock ConStat: Performance-based contamination detection in large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICML 2024}.\n\n\\bibitem[Deng et~al.(2024)]{deng2024investigating}\nDeng, C., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Investigating data contamination in modern benchmarks for large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{NAACL 2024}.\n\n\\bibitem[Dodge et~al.(2021)]{dodge2021documenting}\nDodge, J., et~al. (2021).\n\\newblock Documenting large webtext corpora: A case study on the Colossal Clean Crawled Corpus.\n\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP 2021}.\n\n\\bibitem[Dong et~al.(2024)]{dong2024generalization}\nDong, G., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Generalization or memorization: Data contamination and trustworthy evaluation for large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ACL 2024}.\n\n\\bibitem[Golchin \\& Bansal(2024)]{golchin2024time}\nGolchin, S. and Bansal, M. (2024).\n\\newblock Time travel in LLMs: Tracing data contamination in large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR 2024}.\n\n\\bibitem[Holland \\& Thayer(1988)]{holland1988differential}\nHolland, P.~W. and Thayer, D.~T. (1988).\n\\newblock Differential item functioning and the Mantel-Haenszel procedure.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Test Validity}, pp. 129--145. Erlbaum.\n\n\\bibitem[Jacovi et~al.(2023)]{jacovi2023stop}\nJacovi, A., et~al. (2023).\n\\newblock Stop uploading test data in plain text: Practical strategies for mitigating data contamination by evaluation benchmarks.\n\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP 2023}.\n\n\\bibitem[Kiela et~al.(2021)]{kiela2021dynabench}\nKiela, D., et~al. (2021).\n\\newblock Dynabench: Rethinking benchmarking in NLP.\n\\newblock In \\emph{NAACL 2021}.\n\n\\bibitem[Lalor et~al.(2019)]{lalor2019learning}\nLalor, J.~P., et~al. (2019).\n\\newblock Learning latent parameters without human response patterns: Item response theory with artificial crowds.\n\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP 2019}.\n\n\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2024)]{li2024task}\nLi, Y., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Task contamination: Language models may not be few-shot anymore.\n\\newblock In \\emph{AAAI 2024}.\n\n\\bibitem[Magar \\& Schwartz(2022)]{magar2022data}\nMagar, I. and Schwartz, R. (2022).\n\\newblock Data contamination: From memorization to exploitation.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ACL 2022}.\n\n\\bibitem[Perlitz et~al.(2024)]{perlitz2024metabench}\nPerlitz, Y., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock METABENCH: A large-scale meta-evaluation of LLM benchmarks.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.12844}.\n\n\\bibitem[Polo et~al.(2024)]{polo2024tinybenchmarks}\nPolo, F.~M., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock tinyBenchmarks: Evaluating LLMs with fewer examples.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICML 2024}.\n\n\\bibitem[Rodriguez et~al.(2021)]{rodriguez2021evaluation}\nRodriguez, P., et~al. (2021).\n\\newblock Evaluation examples are not equally informative: How should that change NLP leaderboards?\n\\newblock In \\emph{ACL 2021}.\n\n\\bibitem[Shi et~al.(2024)]{shi2024detecting}\nShi, W., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock Detecting pretraining data from large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR 2024}.\n\n\\bibitem[Vania et~al.(2021)]{vania2021comparing}\nVania, C., et~al. (2021).\n\\newblock Comparing test sets with item response theory.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ACL 2021}.\n\n\\bibitem[Yang et~al.(2023)]{yang2023rethinking}\nYang, S., et~al. (2023).\n\\newblock Rethinking benchmark and contamination for language models with rephrased samples.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2311.04850}.\n\n\\bibitem[Zhu et~al.(2024)]{zhu2024dyval}\nZhu, K., et~al. (2024).\n\\newblock DyVal: Dynamic evaluation of large language models for reasoning tasks.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR 2024}.\n\n\\bibitem[Zhuang et~al.(2023)]{zhuang2023efficiently}\nZhuang, Y., et~al. (2023).\n\\newblock Efficiently measuring the cognitive ability of LLMs: An adaptive testing perspective.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2306.10014}.\n\n\\bibitem[Zumbo(1999)]{zumbo1999handbook}\nZumbo, B.~D. (1999).\n\\newblock \\emph{A Handbook on the Theory and Methods of Differential Item Functioning}.\n\\newblock National Defense Headquarters, Ottawa.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n%% ========================================================================\n%% APPENDIX\n%% ========================================================================\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Experiments}\n\n\\subsection{RL Robustness Analysis}\n\\label{app:rl}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF stability across cohort compositions.}\n\\label{tab:rl}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Cohort} & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{Jaccard vs.\\ SFT} & \\textbf{ConStat AUROC} \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT baseline & 31.8\\% & -- & 0.72 \\\\\n$+$ Alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO) & 30.5\\% & 0.87 & 0.68 \\\\\n$+$ Reasoning-RL ($N = 13$) & 31.1\\% & 0.92 & 0.49 \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multicolumn{4}{l}{\\textit{Traditional detectors on reasoning-RL:}} \\\\\nMin-K\\% AUROC & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{0.44} \\\\\nDIF bootstrap AUROC [95\\% CI] & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{[0.88, 0.95]} \\\\\nDeLong DIF vs.\\ ConStat & \\multicolumn{3}{c}{$p = 0.002$} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:rl} shows DIF stability when varying focal cohort composition.\nAdding alignment-RL models (DPO/ORPO) changes C\\% minimally (31.8\\%$\\to$30.5\\%, Jaccard\\,=\\,0.87).\nIncluding 13 reasoning-RL models (GRPO/PPO) has negligible impact (C\\%\\,=\\,31.1\\%, Jaccard\\,=\\,0.92), as expected given they comprise only 1.6\\% of the focal group.\nTraditional detectors show degraded performance on reasoning-RL models (ConStat AUROC: 0.49; Min-K\\%: 0.44), while DIF maintains high AUROC [0.88, 0.95].\nThe $N = 13$ sample is insufficient for definitive claims about RL robustness; this remains a priority for future work with larger reasoning-RL model collections.\n\n\\subsection{GSM8K Cross-Benchmark Replication}\n\\label{app:gsm8k}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM8K DIF analysis: temporal DIF, semi-synthetic validation, and GSM1K correlation.}\n\\label{tab:gsm8k}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Standard matching} & \\textbf{External matching} \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal C\\% & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{18.9\\% (random: 0.3\\%)} \\\\\nSpearman (standard vs.\\ external) & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{0.926} \\\\\n$\\Delta$TPR at $p = 1.0$ & 0.818 & 0.517 \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\textit{GSM1K correlation (exploratory, $N = 33$):}} \\\\\nPearson $r$ & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$-0.478$ ($p = 0.014$)} \\\\\nSpearman $\\rho$ & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$-0.403$} \\\\\nPartial $r$ (controlling accuracy) & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$-0.385$} \\\\\nLeave-one-out $r$ range & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$[-0.537, -0.374]$} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nGSM8K temporal DIF (C\\%\\,=\\,18.9\\%) falls within the cross-benchmark range and confirms framework portability (Table~\\ref{tab:gsm8k}).\nThe semi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR is higher under standard matching (+0.818) than external matching (+0.517), reflecting GSM8K's single-domain structure where external matching is effectively total-score matching on a different benchmark.\nThe GSM1K contamination gap shows a negative correlation with DIF ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.014$, $N = 33$): models with more DIF items show \\emph{smaller} contamination gaps.\nThis counter-intuitive result supports the meta-critique that DIF captures differential functioning orthogonal to web-overlap contamination.\nHowever, $N = 33$ with wide confidence intervals means this finding is exploratory and requires replication.\n\n\\subsection{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\\label{app:bayes}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Detection power by number of cumulative administrations and effect size.}\n\\label{tab:bayes}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Effect size $\\delta$} & \\textbf{1 admin} & \\textbf{2 admin} & \\textbf{3 admin} & \\textbf{4 admin} \\\\\n\\midrule\n$\\delta = 1.0$ & 0.42 & 0.58 & 0.69 & 0.74 \\\\\n$\\delta = 1.4$ & 0.68 & -- & 0.85 & -- \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multicolumn{5}{l}{Cumulative FPR: 0.048} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:bayes} shows that cumulative Bayesian DIF priors improve detection power for medium-effect items ($\\delta = 1.0$) from 0.42 (single administration) to 0.74 (four administrations) while maintaining FPR at 0.048.\nLarge-effect items ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) already achieve adequate power in a single administration (0.68), so the marginal benefit of accumulation is smaller.\nIf benchmark maintainers adopt quarterly DIF audits, cumulative priors would enable detection of subtler temporal shifts over multiple release cycles.\n\n\\subsection{Graceful Degradation Analysis}\n\\label{app:degrade}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Benchmark validity under progressive item replacement.}\n\\label{tab:degrade}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Replacement \\%} & \\textbf{Rank $\\rho$} & \\textbf{Reliability $\\alpha$} & \\textbf{Construct coverage} \\\\\n\\midrule\n5\\% & 0.9991 & 0.982 & Maintained \\\\\n10\\% & 0.9978 & 0.979 & Maintained \\\\\n20\\% & 0.9943 & 0.971 & Maintained \\\\\n30\\% & -- & -- & Maintained \\\\\n40\\% & -- & -- & Degraded ($\\rho < 0.98$) \\\\\n50\\% & 0.9712 & 0.938 & Partially maintained \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:degrade} shows that MMLU rankings degrade gracefully under item replacement.\nEven at 20\\% replacement, rank correlation remains $\\rho = 0.9943$ and reliability $\\alpha = 0.971$.\nConstruct coverage is maintained up to 30\\% replacement; the critical degradation threshold is approximately 40\\%.\nThese results quantify the massive measurement redundancy in MMLU's 12,508 items and support the ``instability $\\neq$ invalidity'' framing: the benchmark can tolerate substantial item turnover without compromising its measurement function.\n\n\\subsection{Item-Focused Trees}\n\\label{app:ift}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Item-Focused Trees vs.\\ MH-DIF concordance.}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} \\\\\n\\midrule\nItems with significant temporal split & 4,287 (34.3\\%) \\\\\nConcordance with MH C-class & 0.81 \\\\\nJaccard (IFT vs.\\ MH C) & 0.74 \\\\\nItems detected only by IFT & 812 \\\\\nItems detected only by MH & 443 \\\\\nMedian primary split date & 2023-09-15 \\\\\nIQR of split dates & 2023-06 to 2024-01 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nItem-Focused Trees \\citep{zumbo1999handbook}, which treat release date as a continuous covariate, detect 34.3\\% of items with significant temporal splits (Table~\\ref{tab:ift}).\nThe 81\\% concordance with binary MH-DIF validates the 2023/2024 binary split as a reasonable first approximation.\nThe 812 items detected only by IFT likely exhibit function shifts at non-boundary time points (median split: September 2023, coinciding with GPT-4 and Llama-2 release timelines).\nIFT provides a finer-grained temporal view, but the high concordance confirms that binary MH captures the dominant signal.\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
